# React Rendering Optimization

## Overview
Excessive re-renders are a common cause of performance issues in React applications.

## Key Techniques

### 1. React.memo for Functional Components
```typescript
const ProductCard = React.memo(({ product }) => {
  return <div>{product.name}</div>;
}, (prevProps, nextProps) => {
  // Return true if props are equal (skip render)
  return prevProps.product.id === nextProps.product.id;
});
```

### 2. useMemo Hook
```typescript
const expensiveValue = useMemo(() => {
  return computeExpensiveValue(a, b);
}, [a, b]); // Only recompute when a or b changes
```

### 3. useCallback Hook
```typescript
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething(a, b);
}, [a, b]); // Function reference stays same if deps unchanged
```

### 4. Avoid Inline Functions/Objects
```typescript
// BAD - creates new function on every render
<Component onClick={() => doSomething()} />

// GOOD - memoized function
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(), []);
<Component onClick={handleClick} />
```

### 5. Code Splitting and Lazy Loading
```typescript
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';

const HeavyComponent = dynamic(() => import('./HeavyComponent'), {
  loading: () => <p>Loading...</p>
});
```

### 6. Virtual Scrolling for Long Lists
For lists with 100+ items, use virtual scrolling to render only visible items.

## Debugging Tools
- React DevTools Profiler
- Chrome DevTools Performance tab
- next/image for image optimization

